course: dictionary
lesson: 11762
title: Words from long ago
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A bard was a poet and singer who told stories in verse, which means in poem-style lines. Bards lived long ago in Celtic lands such as Wales, Ireland and Scotland. You can use the clues around a word to work out its meaning. «The old bard sang of brave heroes while he played his harp.» The clues are sang, harp, stories and old, so a bard is a musician-storyteller.
explain: Today, bard can also mean any poet, so a bard is a poet who writes verse. When it has a capital letter, «The Bard» means William Shakespeare. People call him «the Bard of Avon», after the River Avon where he was born. Check the word by swapping it into the sentence: does "poet" still make sense? Then you know the meaning.
fact: A bard was a poet and singer who told stories in verse.
fact: Bards lived in old Celtic lands such as Wales, Ireland and Scotland.
fact: Bards kept the stories and family histories of chiefs, often singing them with a harp.
fact: «The Bard» with a capital letter means William Shakespeare.
fact: In Wales, the Eisteddfod still crowns a bard.
exercise: «The old bard sang of brave heroes while he played his harp.» What is a bard? | A knight who fought in battles | A king who ruled the land | *A poet and singer who told stories | A farmer who grew barley
exercise: «People call William Shakespeare the Bard of Avon.» Who does «the Bard» mean here? | The River Avon | A chief from Scotland | *William Shakespeare | A harp player in a band
exercise: «In Wales, the Eisteddfod still crowns a bard.» Which country is this festival in? | Spain | *Wales | Egypt | Japan
test: What did bards keep safe in old times? | Maps of the sea | Coins from faraway lands | Fresh bread recipes | *The stories and family histories of chiefs
test: What instrument did bards often sing with? | A drum | A trumpet | A piano | *A harp
exam: Read: «The bard told a tale of brave heroes in old Wales.» Which word means the same as bard? | Gardener | Pirate | Captain | *Poet
